<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miroslav Gospel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1180–<a href="/years/1190">1190</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Miroslav Gospel is an illuminated Cyrillic gospel book written around 1180–1190 for Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miroslav-of-hum">Miroslav of Hum</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a>. Richly decorated with initials combining Byzantine and Romanesque motifs, it is one of the oldest surviving documents in the Serbian recension of Old Church Slavonic. Kept in the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, it is listed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Memory of the World register.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miroslav-gospel.jpg" alt="Miroslav Gospel" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonimus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miroslav%27s_Gospel_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bohemian was used in the Czech lands until 1097; the Croatian kept the angular Glagolitic; the Serbian, whose <b>Miroslav Gospel</b> dates to 1180, used Cyrillic alone from the 1200s; and the Russian emerged in the 1000s from the Eastern Bulgarian.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-church-slavonic">Old Church Slavonic · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince Miroslav, brother of Stefan Nemanja, ruled it in the late 12th century and commissioned the <b>Miroslav Gospel</b>, a masterpiece of Cyrillic illumination.</blockquote><a href="/state/zachlumia">Zachlumia</a></li></ul></section>
